-x, or a weak pair),\n- Or they’re slow-playing a strong hand (like two pair or a set).\n\nBut given they raised pre-flop (200), then checked the flop and turn, they likely have a weaker range now — perhaps top pair with a weak kicker, or missed entirely.\n\nNow, **you have initiative** and can choose to:\n- **Check back (0)**: See the river for free. You’ll get to see the last card without investing more. But you give opponent a free chance to outdraw you or bluff later.\n- **Raise (e.g., 100 or more)**: Represent strength. You may be perceived as having a made hand (like two pair, a set, or even a flush). This could make the opponent fold weaker pairs or draws. It also builds the pot if you hit your straight on the river.\n- **All-in (-2)**: Overkill here. You only have 19,750 chips, and the pot is likely around 400–600 (pre-flop: 200 from opponent, 200 from you; flop and turn checks). Going all-in would be a massive overbet and likely folds out worse or calls with better. Not optimal.\n\nBest move: **Small raise for value and bluff equity**.\n\nSince you have **strong draw equity** (8 outs) and **position/iniative**, a small **raise (100)** can:\n- Build the pot when you hit,\n- Make weaker hands (like A-high, middle pair) fold,\n- Or induce a bluff-catch on the river.\n\nAlso, because opponent showed weakness (checking back the turn), they likely won’t call large bets unless they have something.\n\nRaising **100** is minimal aggression — just double the big blind — and keeps them in the hand if they have a weak pair, maximizing value if you hit.\n\nReason:  \nI have a strong draw with 8 outs to the nuts (or near-nuts), and my opponent has shown weakness by checking twice after a pre-flop raise. A small raise (100) applies pressure, potentially winning the pot immediately, while still keeping the opponent in with weaker hands if they call. Checking would waste the opportunity to build the pot or bluff them off a hand. Raising too much risks folding out all bluffs